612: - FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-001 Context package construction and provenance: the server builds a bounded, reproducible package for a session-log turn from the request, the canonical profile and rules, applicable requirements, related turns, TODOs, receipts, trusted prior responses, and code at the current commit, records source hashes, commit identity, selection rationale, token usage, contradictions, blockers, and omitted candidates, and replays it identically from its stored input snapshot, never from live sources or configuration (BuildAgentContextPackageCommand, GetAgentContextPackageQuery, QueryAgentContextPackagesQuery, ReplayAgentContextPackageQuery).
613: - FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-002 Token budget and explicit overflow: mandatory context is never truncated; when it exceeds the budget the build fails with `agent_context_budget_exceeded`, stores an `Overflow` package for audit, and reports the token counts and mandatory items (BuildAgentContextPackageCommand).
614: - FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-003 Response submission: an agent response is submitted against a built package with its claims and evidence, idempotently by a whole-submission fingerprint (a reused key with any differing input is refused with `conflict`), carrying the package's root ids (SubmitAgentResponseCommand, GetAgentResponseQuery, QueryAgentResponsesQuery).
615: - FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-004 Grading and trust: every response is graded by deterministic evidence checks and by the configured independent model grader, the latter recorded by the HV agent for the responses of an HV request that names a range of session-log turns (no grading worker or HV pipeline in this effort, operator 2026-10-11); a model grade is accepted only from the configured grader, for a response inside an open HV request, with verified jsonl receipts including the reviewer's own rollout, whose recorded runtime model and effort must equal the configured grader's, and only when its scores and findings equal the reviewer's own verdict entry for that response and the reviewer's own session-log turn, from a session independent of the reviewed and the producing ones (the same agent may review), holds that verdict (architecture section 6.3, eight checks); an HV request lists only gradable responses and completes when every item is graded or closed by rejection or supersession; grade, trust, reject, supersede, and staleness writes on one response are serialized; a response is trusted only when both latest grades reach the trust thresholds, never below 98 percent, with zero FAIL and zero UNKNOWN (RunDeterministicGradeCommand, RecordModelGradeCommand, DecideAgentResponseTrustCommand, CreateAgentContextHvRequestCommand, GetAgentContextHvRequestQuery, QueryAgentContextHvRequestsQuery).
616: - FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-005 Rejection, staleness, and supersession: rejected, stale, and superseded responses are preserved for audit and excluded from trusted retrieval; a response becomes stale when a dependency it was built on or cites as evidence changes, and every trusted retrieval checks freshness before returning a response (RejectAgentResponseCommand, SupersedeAgentResponseCommand, EvaluateStalenessCommand).
617: - FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-006 Typed relationships: packages, responses, requirements, turns, TODOs, code files, receipts, profile, and rules are linked with `implements`, `verifies`, `depends_on`, `supports`, `contradicts`, `supersedes`, `blocks`, `derived_from`, and `relevant_to` (CreateAgentContextRelationshipCommand, DeleteAgentContextRelationshipCommand, QueryAgentContextRelationshipsQuery).
618: - FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-007 GraphRAG projection with deterministic fallback: trusted responses are projected into GraphRAG for semantic retrieval and removed when they stop being trusted; retrieval falls back to a deterministic relational path with a stated reason whenever GraphRAG cannot answer, and the relational path ranks by the relationship neighborhood of the turn and focus refs the request names (RetrieveAgentContextCandidatesQuery, ProjectAgentResponsesCommand).
619: - FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-008 Surfaces: every externally callable agent-context unit (GetAgentContextStatusQuery, BuildAgentContextPackageCommand, GetAgentContextPackageQuery, QueryAgentContextPackagesQuery, ReplayAgentContextPackageQuery, SubmitAgentResponseCommand, GetAgentResponseQuery, QueryAgentResponsesQuery, RunDeterministicGradeCommand, RecordModelGradeCommand, RejectAgentResponseCommand, SupersedeAgentResponseCommand, EvaluateStalenessCommand, CreateAgentContextRelationshipCommand, DeleteAgentContextRelationshipCommand, QueryAgentContextRelationshipsQuery, RetrieveAgentContextCandidatesQuery, ProjectAgentResponsesCommand, GetAgentContextPilotReportQuery, CreateAgentContextHvRequestCommand, GetAgentContextHvRequestQuery, QueryAgentContextHvRequestsQuery) is available through REST (`/qbrainai/agent-context/*` and the `/mcpserver` alias), MCP tools, the typed client, the REPL (`client.AgentContext.*`), and the plugin builders and tools of all nine plugins; the two internal units have no endpoint by design: DecideAgentResponseTrustCommand (the trust decision, run inside RunDeterministicGradeCommand and RecordModelGradeCommand) and process RunStatelessInvocation (the plugin's stateless invocation, run by the plugins' prompt hooks).
620: - FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-009 Opt-in mode and pilot: `Mcp:AgentContext:Mode` (`Off` default, `Pilot`, `Stateless`) keeps stateful behavior until the operator changes the default from the pilot report; `Stateless` runs only on a host whose plugin API can exclude conversation history from the model request (P0.2), and any other host treats it as `Pilot` with its feature-matrix limitation (GetAgentContextStatusQuery, process RunStatelessInvocation, GetAgentContextPilotReportQuery).
621: - TR-MCP-NUKE-011 (under FR-MCP-BUILD-001, no TEST record) Targeted test runs through Nuke: `Test` accepts `--test-projects` and `--test-filter`; AC-001 `--test-projects` runs only the named projects among those the target already selects (`NukeTest_TestProjects_RunsOnlyNamed`); AC-002 an unknown project fails before any test runs (`NukeTest_UnknownProject_FailsBeforeRun`); AC-003 `--test-filter` is appended as `(<category filter>)&(<filter>)` (`NukeTest_TestFilter_CombinedWithCategory`); AC-004 without the parameters the target is unchanged (`NukeTest_NoParameters_Unchanged`). Evidence: these Build.Tests cases, run in the Build.Tests infrastructure step.
622: - TR-MCP-NUKE-012 (under FR-MCP-BUILD-001, no TEST record) Plugin core suite through Nuke: `TestPluginCore --pester-paths <paths>` AC-001 runs Pester 5 with `-CI` on the named `plugins/core/test-fixtures/pester` files (`TestPluginCore_RunsPesterCiOnNamedPaths`); AC-002 fails on any failure (`TestPluginCore_PesterFailure_FailsTarget`); AC-003 fails on any skip (`TestPluginCore_PesterSkip_FailsTarget`); AC-004 fails on a missing path (`TestPluginCore_MissingPath_FailsTarget`); AC-005 runs natively on Windows and on Linux (`TestPluginCore_RunsNatively`, run on both). Evidence: these Build.Tests cases.
623: - TR-MCP-NUKE-013 (under FR-MCP-BUILD-001, no TEST record) Agent plugin suites through Nuke: `TestAgentPlugins --agent-plugin-parent <dir> [--plugins <names>]` runs Pester (`Invoke-Pester -Path tests -CI`) in each selected plugin repo, the six PowerShell hosts and the three TypeScript hosts alike, natively, failing on any failure, skip, or missing repo, and on any `*.bats` file or npm or jest test command left in a selected repo (operator, 2026-10-11, PowerShell-only ruling), and writes a per-repo receipt: AC-001 runs Pester in each selected repo (`TestAgentPlugins_RunsPesterPerSelectedRepo`); AC-002 fails on any failure or skip (`TestAgentPlugins_FailureOrSkip_FailsTarget`); AC-003 fails on a missing repo (`TestAgentPlugins_MissingRepo_FailsTarget`); AC-004 fails on a forbidden runner (`TestAgentPlugins_ForbiddenRunnerPresent_FailsTarget`); AC-005 writes the per-repo receipt (`TestAgentPlugins_WritesPerRepoReceipt`). Evidence: these Build.Tests cases.
624: - TR-MCP-NUKE-014 (under FR-MCP-BUILD-001, no TEST record) Nuke runs no Build.Tests (operator, 2026-10-11: "Nuke does not run its own test."): AC-001 `MigrationIntegrationTests` and every other Nuke target exclude `tests/Build.Tests` from their project lists (`NukeTargets_ProjectLists_ExcludeBuildTests`); AC-002 the Build.Tests `Category=Integration` cases still run in the direct Build.Tests infrastructure step (`BuildTestsStep_FullProject_IncludesIntegrationCategory`). Evidence: these Build.Tests cases.
625: 
626: The six agent-context use cases (architecture document sections 7.11 to 7.16) realize these FRs: "Build a context package for a request" FR-MCP-AGENTCONTEXT-001, -002; "Submit and grade an agent response" -003, -004; "Retrieve trusted prior context" -007; "Maintain relationships, staleness, and supersession" -005, -006; "Run a stateless or pilot invocation" -008, -009; "Project responses into GraphRAG" -007.
627: 
628: ## TDD AC allocation by phase
629: 
630: Two kinds of AC. Baseline-green ACs document behavior that exists and passes today; this plan has exactly five (FR-SUPPORT-016 `ac-1`..`ac-4` and TR-SUPPORT-CORE-016 `ac-1`); they need a green receipt from the existing named tests run in P0 (`./build.ps1 Test --test-projects QBrainAi.Support.Mcp.Tests --test-filter "FullyQualifiedName~SessionLogServiceReplaceDeleteTests"`, the Nuke parameters of P0.1a, zero failures, zero skips), no RED, and must stay green at every later gate. Every other AC is new or changed behavior and is assigned to the slice that writes its RED test first; extension slices add the same AC on their surface. Every listed new or changed AC is RED and then mocks-green in Phase 3, before any production code, and green at the gate of the Phase 4 work package that implements it (the slice label names the scope entry whose RED test first covers the AC); the phase HV verifies every new or changed AC of the phase has its RED and green receipts and every baseline-green AC its green receipts.
631: 
632: - P0 (no product tests): creates every requirement, AC, mapping, and use case; AC allocation itself is a P0 HV claim.
633: - Phase A
634:   - A1: SESSIONTURN-004-001, -003; SESSIONTURN-001-001..003 (server mint).
635:   - A2: SESSIONTURN-001-006, -007.
636:   - A3: SESSIONTURN-001-001..004, -008, -009, -010 (plugin); SESSIONTURN-002-001, -002; SESSIONTURN-007-009 (the envelopes of A3's queue, system-entry, and `unsupportedSince` records).
637:   - A4: SESSIONTURN-001-005; SESSIONTURN-002-003; SESSIONTURN-003-001, -002, -004; SESSIONTURN-007-001..008; extensions of SESSIONTURN-001-002 (plugin write target, open list, and ledger), -001-003, -001-009, -001-010, and -002-001 (verb-side halves of the A3 cases, idle revival), and SESSIONTURN-007-009 (every other record family).
